### E.4 Influential Projects & Codebases

The best way to learn idiomatic Zig is to study real, high‑quality codebases. The following projects are exemplars of different facets of the language.

**TigerBeetle**
[github.com/tigerbeetle/tigerbeetle](https://github.com/tigerbeetle/tigerbeetle)
A distributed financial accounting database that holds itself to correctness and performance standards most systems never approach. TigerBeetle demonstrates:

- Comptime‑specialised data structures (e.g., a log‑structured merge tree built from `@typeInfo`‑driven code)
- Custom allocators that track arena boundaries and perform deferred deallocation
- Deterministic simulation testing (the “VOPR”) that fuzzes concurrency behaviour
- The use of `packed struct` for efficient, platform‑portable disk formats

If you read one Zig codebase cover‑to‑cover, make it this one.

**Ghostty**
[github.com/ghostty-org/ghostty](https://github.com/ghostty-org/ghostty)
A GPU‑accelerated terminal emulator. Ghostty showcases how to write a cross‑platform application that integrates with native rendering backends (Metal, OpenGL, Vulkan). Pay attention to:

- The threading model and lock‑free message passing between the render thread and the PTY reader
- How `defer` and `errdefer` guarantee that GPU resources are freed even on error paths
- The build system that orchestrates dependencies (HarfBuzz, FreeType) across platforms

**Zigwin32**
[github.com/marlersoft/zigwin32](https://github.com/marlersoft/zigwin32)
Autogenerated Windows API bindings. This project is a masterclass in comptime metaprogramming: it parses Microsoft’s metadata at compile time and emits precisely the correct Zig declarations, including `extern struct` layouts and `callconv(.Win64)` signatures. The source code will reshape how you think about code generation — it is all plain Zig, no external tool required.

**Capy**
[github.com/capy-ui/capy](https://github.com/capy-ui/capy)
A GUI library that uses Zig’s comptime facilities to define declarative widget trees, then compiles them into efficient runtime drawing calls. It illustrates the “comptime over runtime magic” pillar better than any tutorial.

**Known Folders**
[github.com/ziglibs/known-folders](https://github.com/ziglibs/known-folders)
A small, meticulously written library that returns standard directory paths (XDG, AppData, etc.) across platforms. It exemplifies:

- How to accept an `Allocator` parameter and return error unions
- The use of `std.os` APIs with proper error handling
- Structuring a library for reuse via `build.zig.zon`

**ZLS (Zig Language Server)**
[github.com/zigtools/zls](https://github.com/zigtools/zls)
The official language server, written in Zig. It is a real‑world example of how to parse Zig with the self‑hosted compiler API, maintain a concurrent request handler, and interface with editor protocols. The codebase demonstrates `std.Thread` usage, arena allocators for per‑request scratch memory, and the delicate art of providing a responsive tool under memory constraints.

**Bun**
[github.com/oven-sh/bun](https://github.com/oven-sh/bun)
A JavaScript runtime built with Zig and C++/JavaScriptCore. While much of Bun is not pure Zig, its Zig portions (HTTP client, bundler, transpiler) show how to write high‑throughput network code with direct syscalls, how to manage an arena of native memory for JavaScript objects, and how to create a C API from Zig that can be consumed by the V8 or JSC embedding layers.

**River**
[github.com/riverwm/river](https://github.com/riverwm/river)
A dynamic tiling Wayland compositor in Zig. It demonstrates an event‑loop architecture built on a custom `std.event`‑style poll loop, FFI bindings to `libwayland`, and a clean separation of concerns between input handling, layout logic, and rendering.

